 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-021\", \"clauses\": {\"describe_position\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 is a describe-position task: a row of objects, one object at number {n}, asking which word describes its position.\"}, \"objects\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 names the objects directly through objects_plural and object_singular, drawn from the generator's own object list (basket, egg, mango and so on).\"}, \"ordinal_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F17\", \"template\": \"What ordinal describes position number {n}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 asks for the ordinal that describes a numbered position, so the question is about ordinal numbers. Its n domain is 1 to 10 and its answers are ordinal symbols from F25.\"}, \"1st\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"Which position comes after {symbol}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 prints symbol values that include 1st, 2nd and 3rd, drawn from the repeated generated value symbol, so it addresses these ordinals directly. The keyed answer is derived from symbol per value_derivations.\"}, \"2nd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"Which position comes after {symbol}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 prints 2nd among the symbol values (1st through 9th).\"}, \"3rd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"Which position comes after {symbol}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 prints 3rd among the symbol values (1st through 9th).\"}, \"up_to_10th\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F24\", \"template\": \"{lineup} stand in a row. Who is {symbol} in the row?\", \"reasoning\": \"F24 prints symbol values that include 10th, the top of the declared ordinal_range of 1 to 10, so it reaches the upper bound of the clause.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F12\", \"F14\", \"F16\", \"F20\", \"F21\", \"F22\", \"F25\", \"F31\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"The number {n} in order is called the {symbol} ({word}).\", \"The {lineup} are lined up on a shelf from left to right. Which one is in {symbol} place?\", \"The {symbol} ({word}) place is position number {n}.\", \"Who arrived earlier: the student in {symbol} place or {symbol2} place?\", \"Write the symbol for the {word} position.\", \"{capitalize} = position {n}, written as {symbol}.\", \"{n}{v}\", \"{w}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"(1) F14 fails sentence_max_words (15). Its lineup slot is nested in F23, whose head can be F29, which prints 8 distinct object names joined with 'and' plus a last item. The first sentence of F14 then runs about 21 words, well over the declared limit of 15. F24 stays under 15 words on the same path. (2) F20 fails. Its symbol2 slot is nested in F25, F32 and F33. F32 and F33 print student names and object names, so the question can read 'the student in' followed by a name or object instead of an ordinal. This is wrong for a 1st-10th lc. (3) F25 '{n}{v}' fails. The n slot (1 to 10) and the v slot (nd, rd, st, th) have no derived_from_values link between them in the packet, so suffixes can pair with the wrong number (for example a suffix that does not match the ordinal). Keyed answers and distractors from F25 can therefore be incorrect ordinals. (4) F31 '{w}' fails. Its _ORDINAL_WORDS basis includes eleventh through twentieth, beyond the declared ordinal_range of 1 to 10, and F31 appears in correct_answer and distractors. It is also the word family used for 'Which word describes...' answers, and no derivation ties those words to n. (5) F12, F16, F21 and F22 fail. Each prints a word from the 'word' key alongside a symbol from the 'symbol' key. value_derivations derives symbol from n and answer from symbol, but no derivation ties 'word' to n or symbol. A render can therefore print a word that disagrees with the ordinal beside it, for example a hint saying the 3rd is 'first', or a keyed answer that does not match the word in the question. The packet gives no basis to exclude these mismatches. Rule 2 is satisfied for all clauses, with families chosen so each clause is produced by a family whose own text is not among the failing families.\"}",
